--TEST--
`Core\Request::query` answers the same way for every name it is given when no request arrived, so nothing about the parameter reaches the decision -- ADR 0012 § 7, spec §§ 9, 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep, counted rather than read off a line: `query` is the
// one of the three request-line readers that takes an argument, so it is the
// one that could answer differently for one name than for another. It must
// not. Whether a request arrived is decided before the name is looked at, and a
// member that reported "no such parameter" for one spelling and refused for
// another would be telling a caller something about a query string that does
// not exist.
uint $refused = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::query("id");
} catch (LogicError $ordinary) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::query("");
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::query("a[b]");
} catch (LogicError $bracketed) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::query("a name with spaces and %20 in it");
} catch (LogicError $unspellable) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
echo "every name refused: ", $refused, " of 4\n";

// The message is the same one for all four, which is the counted claim stated
// the other way: it names the member and never the name, so no spelling of a
// parameter can be told apart from another by reading it.
try {
    Core\Request::query("a[b]");
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}

// And `query` refuses on the same terms as the two readers that take no
// argument at all -- the whole class is unavailable, not this one member.
uint $siblings = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::method();
} catch (LogicError $verb) {
    $siblings = $siblings + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::path();
} catch (LogicError $where) {
    $siblings = $siblings + 1;
}
echo "its two siblings refused: ", $siblings, " of 2\n";
--EXPECT--
every name refused: 4 of 4
Core\Request::query(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
its two siblings refused: 2 of 2
